<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/18th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Kunersdorf</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1759">1759</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seven-years-war">Seven Years' War</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of Kunersdorf was fought on 12 August 1759 near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankfurt">Frankfurt</a> an der Oder in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seven-years-war">Seven Years' War</a>. A Russian army under Pyotr Saltykov and an Austrian corps under Ernst Gideon von Laudon crushed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-ii">Frederick II</a>'s Prussian army, which lost about half its men. Frederick briefly despaired, but the allies failed to follow up their victory.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-kunersdorf.jpg" alt="Battle of Kunersdorf" width="320" height="201" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Alexander von Kotzebue · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Alexander_von_Kotzebue_-_Battle_of_Kunersdorf_on_1_August_1759.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The battle belonged to the Third Silesian War and the wider <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seven-years-war">Seven Years' War</a>. By 1759 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> had reached a strategic defensive position, with Russian and Austrian troops around it. After a Prussian corps was defeated at the Battle of Kay on 23 July, Pyotr Saltykov's Russians occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankfurt">Frankfurt</a> an der Oder on 31 July and entrenched near Kunersdorf, and an Austrian corps under Ernst Gideon von Laudon joined them on 3 to 5 August. By 9 August Frederick the Great had 49,000 to 50,000 men. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allies">Allies</a> numbered about 41,000 Russians and 18,500 Austrians, and the terrain suited defence better than attack.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>Frederick crossed the Oder and marched round the Russian flank, a trek of up to eight hours, sending no reconnaissance. Saltykov had meanwhile reversed his line. At 11:30 on 12 August the Prussian artillery opened fire. Frederick's troops took the Mühlberge, but against the advice of his brother Prince Henry and other generals, who pointed to heat and exhaustion, he chose to press on.</p><p>A cavalry charge led by Friedrich Wilhelm von Seydlitz against the Russian centre failed and Seydlitz was badly wounded. Laudon led the Austrian cavalry in a counterattack, and fresh Austrian infantry joined the fight at about 7 pm. The Prussians broke and fled, throwing away their weapons.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>That evening Frederick wrote a despairing letter in a peasant hut at Reitwein and named his brother as generalissimo. Allied losses were 16,332, some 27 percent. Sources differ on Prussian losses: Duffy gives 6,000 killed and 13,000 wounded, and others 18,609 or 21,000. The Prussians lost 172 of their own <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cannon">cannon</a>, their horse artillery and 60 percent of their cavalry. Only 3,000 men stayed with Frederick at first, but within days most of the scattered survivors returned, and his army recovered to 32,000 men and 50 guns. Saltykov and Laudon disagreed and did not march on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a>, 80 kilometres away. Military historians generally regard Kunersdorf as Frederick's worst defeat.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of Kunersdorf” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the Seven Years’ War (1756–1763) Prussia, backed only by Britain and Hanover, fought Austria, France, Russia and Sweden, winning at Rossbach and Leuthen in 1757 but suffering crushing defeats such as <b>Kunersdorf</b> in 1759; it survived when Russia left the war after Empress Elizabeth died in 1762.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-prussia">Kingdom of Prussia · Military</a></li></ul></section>
